  <h1>Judiciary Cases Backlog — E-Courts and Technology Interventions</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024–25 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India’s judiciary is one of the world’s largest, but it faces a crippling backlog of cases. As of 2024, more than 5 crore cases were pending across Supreme Court, High Courts, and subordinate courts. This backlog undermines the principle of “justice delayed is justice denied.” The Indian Express examined how E-Courts and digital reforms are being advanced as solutions to improve efficiency and access.
________________________________________
The Problem of Backlog
•	Supreme Court: Over 80,000 cases pending (2024).
•	High Courts: Nearly 60 lakh pending cases.
•	Subordinate Courts: Constitute ~90% of backlog, largely civil and criminal disputes.
•	Systemic Causes:
o	Shortage of judges (vacancy ~25–30%).
o	Frequent adjournments.
o	Procedural delays, inadequate infrastructure.
o	Rising litigation due to economic expansion and rights awareness.
________________________________________
E-Courts and Digital Reforms
1.	E-Courts Project (Phase III, 2023–27)
o	Aimed at “digital by default” judiciary.
o	Interlinked digital case management systems across states.
o	Virtual Courts: For traffic challans and petty offenses.
2.	National Judicial Data Grid (NJDG)
o	Real-time tracking of pendency and disposal rates.
o	Used as policy tool for case load analysis.
3.	Artificial Intelligence (AI) Applications
o	Supreme Court’s SUPACE (Supreme Court Portal for Assistance in Court Efficiency) introduced in 2021.
o	AI used for legal research and case summarization.
4.	Video-Conferencing Infrastructure
o	Widely adopted during COVID-19; now institutionalized for routine hearings.
________________________________________
Challenges in Implementation
•	Digital Divide: Rural litigants and small lawyers face access barriers.
•	Training Gaps: Judges and court staff need digital literacy.
•	Privacy & Security: Concerns about sensitive data stored online.
•	Resistance to Change: Some lawyers prefer traditional systems due to fee structures and familiarity.
________________________________________
Relevance for UPSC
•	GS-II (Polity & Governance): Judicial reforms, access to justice, separation of powers.
•	GS-III (Technology): Role of digital solutions in governance.
•	GS-IV (Ethics): Justice as a moral value; “justice delayed is justice denied.”
•	Essay:
o	“Technology as an enabler of justice.”
o	“Judicial backlog and democracy’s credibility.”
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Fill Vacancies: Strengthen appointments under Collegium/NJAC reforms.<br/>2.	Invest in Infrastructure: Dedicated funds for E-Courts at district level.<br/>3.	Capacity-Building: Digital training for judges, lawyers, and staff.<br/>4.	Hybrid Justice: Blend physical hearings with digital tools to maximize efficiency.<br/>5.	Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR): Encourage mediation and arbitration to reduce litigation load.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s judicial backlog represents a serious governance and rights challenge. The E-Courts initiative, NJDG, and AI tools hold promise, but their success depends on bridging digital divides and overcoming institutional inertia. For UPSC aspirants, this issue links governance, technology, and ethics — demonstrating how justice reforms are central to both democracy and development.</p>
